Minimal change, all in authoring.router.ts:

1. Delete resolveSignerName and its docblock. Keep the docblock's history paragraph by moving it onto the new helper. Import resolveSignerIdentity and SignerNotAttributableError from '../services/part11/resolve-signer-identity.js'.

2. Add one local adapter. It does not repeat the lookup; it maps the canonical result onto this table's rule that signer_name is NULL rather than the email:
```ts
async function authoringSignerName(client: PoolClient, req: Request, res: Response, command: string): Promise<{ name: string | null } | null> {
  try {
    const id = await resolveSignerIdentity(client, Number(getActorId(req)), getTenantId(req), command);
    const name = id.name?.trim();
    return { name: name && name.toLowerCase() !== id.email.toLowerCase() ? name : null };
  } catch (err) {
    if (err instanceof SignerNotAttributableError) { res.status(403).json({ error: 'Signer identity is not attributable in this organization (§11.100). Nothing was signed.', code: 'ESIGNATURE_SIGNER_NOT_ATTRIBUTABLE' }); return null; }
    console.error('[authoring] signer identity lookup failed:', err instanceof Error ? err.message : err);
    res.status(503).json({ error: 'Your printed name could not be read, so nothing was signed. Try again.', code: 'SIGNER_IDENTITY_UNVERIFIED' });
    return null;
  }
}
```
The 403 code is the one server/routes/esignature.ts:326-330 already returns. The canonical function falls back from name to email; the comparison above turns that fallback back into NULL, which keeps the router's rule and the export's 'no printed name on record' line for a lookup that really returns no name.

