The new private helper sits beside refuseIfFilingDiverged:

```ts
async function refuseIfAuthoringAuthor(client, orgId, userId, authoringDocId) {
  try {
    await assertSignerIsNotAuthor(`authoring-document:${authoringDocId}`, orgId, userId,
      { command: 'sign', meaning: QMS_DOCUMENT_APPROVAL_MEANING, client });
  } catch (e) {
    if (e instanceof SeparationOfDutiesError) throw new QmsApprovalRefusedError('SELF_APPROVAL', 'You wrote part of this procedure in the document editor, so you cannot also approve it. A second person has to review and sign it (21 CFR Part 11 §11.10(d)). Nothing was signed.');
    if (e instanceof SeparationOfDutiesAuthorUnresolvedError) throw new QmsApprovalRefusedError('INVALID_AUTHORSHIP', e.message);
    if (e instanceof SeparationOfDutiesUnverifiedError) throw new QmsContentRefusedError(503, e.code, 'Separation of duties could not be verified, so nothing was signed.');
    throw e;
  }
}
```

The imports come from '../governance/separation-of-duties'. Passing `client` is required: the authorship reads (authoring_documents.created_by, DISTINCT doc_revisions.created_by, and the visible-mutation authoring_audit_trail actors) must run on the signing transaction, after captureQmsAuthoringContent has taken FOR UPDATE on the document and its sections.

